<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian-American Company</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian-American Company was a state-chartered trading company that held a monopoly over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> and governed Russian America from 1799 until 1867. Under managers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-baranov">Alexander Baranov</a> it ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitka">Sitka</a>, extended its posts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-ross">Fort Ross</a>, and forced Native hunters to supply sea otter pelts. Its role ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-american-company.jpg" alt="Russian-American Company" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NuclearVacuum · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Russian-American_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Emperor Paul I chartered the Russian-American Company in the Ukase of 1799. It was formed largely on the basis of the United American Company and was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joint-stock-company">joint-stock company</a>, with the mission of founding settlements in Russian America, trading with the native peoples and carrying out a wider colonisation. A government Correspondent, the first being Nikolai Rezanov, oversaw its affairs, and a board of three directors reported to the tsar and appointed a Chief Manager in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Alexander Andreyevich Baranov was the first Chief Manager and founded Pavlovskaya and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitka">New Archangel</a>, now Sitka, which became operating bases. From 1818 the post went only to officers of the Imperial Russian Navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ukase of 1799 gave the company a monopoly over trade in Russian America south to 55° N. Alexander I's Ukase of 1821 claimed 45°50′ N, later revised to 51° N, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> challenged this, which led to the Russo-American Treaty of 1824 and the Russo-British Treaty of 1825. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alutiiq">Alutiiq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleut">Aleut</a> men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleutian-islands">Aleutian Islands</a> were forcibly conscripted for three-year terms as sea otter hunters, and Baranov supplied Aleut labourers to ten American ships sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> between 1805 and 1812, which gathered over 22,000 pelts. Russian merchants were shut out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> went to Kyakhta through Okhotsk, and the company struggled to find skilled crews.</p><p>In 1812 the company built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-ross">Fort Ross</a> in what is now Sonoma County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, its southernmost outpost, on Spanish and later Mexican territory. A proposal of 1824 to colonise California was rejected, and the company sold the settlement in 1841. In 1817 its agent Georg Anton Schäffer built Fort Elizabeth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and tried to overthrow the Kingdom of Hawaii, an episode called the Schäffer affair. The Russian government took control from the merchants in 1818, and from the 1820s profits fell as fur-bearing animals declined. In 1838 the company leased part of the Alaska Panhandle to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From 1797 to 1821 the company and its forerunner collected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> worth 16 million rubles, including 72,894 sea otters. The Alaska Purchase of 1867 transferred Alaska to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the company's commercial interests were sold to Hutchinson, Kohl &amp; Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, which became the Alaska Commercial Company. The Russian-American Company ceased its commercial activities in 1881. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-ross">Fort Ross</a> is partly reconstructed as an open-air museum, and many names of its chief managers appear as place names in Southeast Alaska.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russian-American Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1799 Emperor Paul I granted a monopoly charter to the <b>Russian-American Company</b>, which was to govern the territory on the state's behalf.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
